    "In agent authentication, rich Verifiable Credentials (rVCs) and basic Verifiable Credentials (bVCs) serve distinct roles.",
    "bVCs encode minimal information primarily to identify an agent as an entity without detailed attributes.",
    "bVCs do not specify roles, capabilities, or authorizations.",
    "bVCs are issued only by the orchestrator of the security domain.",
    "bVCs are issued at the time of deployment to freshly-deployed agents.",
    "The main purpose of bVCs is to enable agents to trustfully identify themselves to peers within the same security domain.",
    "rVCs contain additional identity attributes including specific roles, capabilities, and authorizations of an agent.",
    "rVCs are obtained through an intra-domain attestation process.",
    "A designated authority agent issues rVCs to agents already provisioned with bVCs.",
    "The issuance of rVCs involves mutual authentication between the requesting agent and the issuing agent.",
    "The orchestrator of the security domain holds its own DID outside the ledger.",
    "Only the orchestrator is authorized to issue bVCs.",
    "Designated authority agents within the security domain issue rVCs after successful attestation.",
    "rVCs allow for a more flexible and detailed representation of an agent's identity and capabilities.",
    "rVCs can be tailored to reflect specific roles and authorizations of the agent.",
    "The key differences between rVCs and bVCs lie in the depth of information they provide and the authority responsible for their issuance.",
    "bVCs provide basic identification.",
    "rVCs offer a richer, more detailed representation of an agent's roles and capabilities.",
    "rVCs are issued through a more complex attestation process."
] 

