    "The process of applying normalized embeddings to iteratively adjust Lloyd-Max codebook values during fitting is detailed in the paper "Low-Bit N-Gram Embeddings for Language Models" (arXiv:2610.31646v1).",
    "In the Lloyd-Max codebook fitting procedure, embeddings are first normalized to ensure differences in magnitude do not dominate the shared codebook.",
    "Normalization involves calculating a scale for each embedding defined as the root mean square (RMS) of the embedding's components.",
    "For an embedding r, the normalization scale s^(0)_r is computed.",
    "Each component w_ri of the embedding is normalized to produce z_ri by dividing w_ri by s^(0)_r.",
    "The Lloyd-Max algorithm proceeds with two main steps in each iteration.",
    "First, every sampled normalized component is assigned to its nearest codebook value based on distances between normalized components and current codebook values.",
    "The indices of sampled components assigned to each codebook value are stored in sets S_k.",
    "Second, codebook values are updated by replacing each codebook value c_k with the mean of the components assigned to it.",
    "The new codebook value c_k^new is computed as the average of z_j for all j in S_k.",
    "If no components are assigned to a codebook value, that value remains unchanged.",
    "This iterative process is repeated for a total of 60 iterations.",
    "The iterative adjustment allows the codebook to adapt effectively to the distribution of normalized components.",
    "Normalization and iterative adjustment of codebook values are crucial for better fitting of embeddings.",
    "These steps help refine the representation of embeddings in low-bit format while minimizing reconstruction error.",
    "The reconstruction error minimization is discussed in Section A.2 and Section A.3 of the paper."
